<article class="env-lemma" id="05A7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05A7">Lemma <span data-tag="05A7">10.95.4</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be a ring map, let $M$ be an $R$-module, and let $Q$ be a countably generated $S$-submodule of $M \otimes _ R S$. Then there exists a countably generated $R$-submodule $P$ of $M$ such that $\mathop{\mathrm{Im}}(P \otimes _ R S \to M \otimes _ R S)$ contains $Q$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $y_1, y_2, \ldots $ be generators for $Q$ and write $y_ j = \sum _ k x_{jk} \otimes s_{jk}$ for some $x_{jk} \in M$ and $s_{jk} \in S$. Then take $P$ be the submodule of $M$ generated by the $x_{jk}$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>